          "text": "{\"relation\":\"REFINED\",\"consensus_summary\":\"Justice is God’s righteous moral order expressed in impartial judgment, equitable conduct, restraint or punishment of wrongdoing, and protection or restoration of the vulnerable.\",\"book_summary\":\"Justice is righteousness applied specifically to righting a wronged neighbor—the right way of life—and may translate the same Hebrew term rendered elsewhere as “righteousness.”\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Righteous ordering that judges rightly and establishes or restores what is due in human relationships.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Righteous action directed toward restoring a neighbor who has suffered wrong.\",\"core_relation\":\"BOOK_NARROWS\",\"extension_relation\":\"BOOK_SUBSET\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The book centers justice on restorative action for a wronged neighbor. The consensus also includes cases such as impartial adjudication, distributive fairness, punishment of wrongdoing, divine judgment, and broader covenantal moral order even when no particular wronged neighbor is being restored.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"There is no direct contradiction at the center: righting a wronged neighbor is a recognized instance of biblical justice. The material difference is the book’s narrower boundary, which may exclude or subordinate judicial, retributive, and broader ordering functions admitted by the consensus.\",\"rationale\":\"The book retains the consensus category of righteousness expressed in right human relations and especially overlaps its restorative and vulnerable-protecting dimensions. It does not replace that referent with a different one, but defines it more specifically as righteousness applied to repairing an inflicted wrong. That makes the entry a substantive narrowing rather than a match or divergence.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"The cited texts support the close association of justice, righteousness, and the way of the Lord, while Jeremiah 22:3 directly concerns rescuing those who have been wronged. However, the references do not by themselves establish that righting a wronged neighbor exhausts or centrally defines justice; Psalm 89:14 and 97:2 instead present justice as a broad attribute of God’s rule.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
      ],
